Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "programming-languages": softwareArticle({
    slug:"programming-languages",title:"Programming languages",eyebrow:"Comparative software history · 1944–1985",infoboxKicker:"International technical field",
    lead:"Programming languages in the postwar world develop inside five durable computing cultures. Germany treats a program as a certified plan; Britain and the Commonwealth as an information service; the United States as a replaceable general tool; Japan and the Co-Prosperity Sphere as a controlled procedure; and the Latin bloc as an intelligible instrument.",
    canon:"The five cultures, principal language lineages, generational chronology, and hardware constraints are established through 1985. Exact formal grammars and complete standard libraries remain matters for the individual standards bodies.",
    sections:[
      {id:"cultures",title:"The five computing cultures",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Governing conception</th><th>Characteristic lineages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Germany</td><td>Computer as certified plan</td><td>Plankalkül, Rechenplan, Planform, Kurzplan</td></tr><tr><td>Commonwealth</td><td>Computer as information service</td><td>Autocode, CLEO, CPL, BCPL, ML, occam</td></tr><tr><td>United States</td><td>Computer as replaceable general tool</td><td>FORTRAN, COBOL, C, Lambda, Civic</td></tr><tr><td>Japan and GEACPS</td><td>Computer as controlled procedure</td><td>KEISAN, TEJUN, Gakushū</td></tr><tr><td>Latin bloc</td><td>Computer as intelligible instrument</td><td>ALGOL-Latin, LIA, Marseille Prolog</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"generations",title:"Generational development",html:"<p>Relay and valve machines begin with switches, plugboards, numeric orders, tape, and hand-planned storage. Magnetic core, batch monitors, and compilers make higher-level scientific and administrative languages practical during the 1950s. Disks, interrupts, terminals, and compatible families permit modules, exceptions, persistent files, and time sharing in the 1960s.</p><p>Microprocessors and ROM interpreters carry programming into homes after 1975. Packet networks and sixteen- or thirty-two-bit personal systems make interfaces, gateways, and software portability the central questions of the early 1980s.</p>"},
      {id:"media",title:"Source media and programmer practice",html:"<p>Programs move from panels and punched tape to cards, teleprinters, interactive terminals, cassettes, disks, and graphical workstations. An old language does not retroactively acquire a new medium: early Rechenplan, FORTRAN, KEISAN, and LIA remain batch languages even after descendants become interactive.</p><p>Professional roles also diverge. Scientists, systems analysts, coding offices, operators, library custodians, test engineers, and certification reviewers may all contribute to one executable without sharing the same source notation.</p>"},
      {id:"objects",title:"Object programs, monitors, and runtimes",html:"<p>Early compilers emit relocatable routines and loader instructions. By the 1960s, named modules carry external-symbol and relocation tables; by the late 1970s, native objects coexist with intermediate forms and compact bytecode.</p><p>A language is therefore inseparable from its monitor, linker, runtime, file conventions, and device libraries. Similar syntax does not imply binary compatibility, while a stable intermediate form can sometimes join machines that share no instruction set.</p>"},
      {id:"characters",title:"Character identity",html:"<p>Germany's six- and twelve-bit order, America's ASCII–EBCDIC division, Japanese compact and multibyte registries, British machine diversity, and the Latin eight-bit system make text a portability problem in its own right. Source character, displayed glyph, interchange identity, and machine representation are distinct technical facts.</p><p><a href='unicode-80.html'>UNICODE 80</a> supplies common sixteen-bit abstract identities after 1980, but it does not impose one processor word, file encoding, collation, or programming language.</p>"},
      {id:"exchange",title:"Survival and exchange",html:"<p>Assembly remains universal. FORTRAN, COBOL, ALGOL, LISP, BASIC, Pascal, Simula, C, Prolog, ML, and Ada cross bloc lines through journals, licenses, translators, universities, contractors, and technical diplomacy.</p><p>Borrowing rarely erases local institutions. German C-like work still calls Zivil-Planform services; Japanese languages retain native vertical representation; Latin implementations emphasize readable errors and multilingual records; Commonwealth ports arrive with formal deviation reports.</p>"},
      {id:"endpoint",title:"The field in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 no single language dominates all work. FORTRAN and KEISAN remain scientific; COBOL, CLEO, LIA, and TEJUN administer institutions; Rechenplan and Ada-class systems certify strategic machines; C, BCPL, and Werkcode build systems; BASIC, Kurzplan, and Gakushū teach and entertain; ML, Lambda, and Prolog define research frontiers.</p><p>The important division is not high-level versus low-level, but who controls the compiler, runtime, interfaces, distribution, and right to connect a program to a regulated machine.</p>"}
    ],facts:[["Reference period","1944–1985"],["Durable computing cultures","Five"],["First media","Panels, paper tape, and cards"],["Consumer-language expansion","After 1975"],["Shared character standard","UNICODE 80"],["Universal binary standard","None"],["Principal portability form","Source plus declared environment"]]
  }),

  "rechenplan": softwareArticle({
    slug:"rechenplan",title:"Rechenplan",eyebrow:"German programming-language family · 1952–1985",infoboxKicker:"German national language family",
    lead:"Rechenplan is Germany's principal scientific, administrative, and control-programming family. Descended from Konrad Zuse's Plankalkül and postwar stored-program research, it joins strong static typing to national intermediate forms, certified compilers, and a documentary culture in which a program is a signed and reproducible plan.",
    canon:"The 1952, 1956, 1963, 1968, and 1972 revisions, three profiles, Planform route, and certification role are established. Exact grammars and machine-specific libraries vary by revision and target.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Plankalkül inheritance",html:"<p>Plankalkül supplies typed quantities, arrays, structured plans, and the claim that a calculation can be described independently of relay wiring. After 1947 its manuscript notation is linearized for cards and tape, while input and output are separated from mathematical expressions.</p><p>German practice already distinguishes the scientist, <em>Planer</em>, machine coder, operator, and maintenance engineer. Rechenplan grows by mechanizing this chain rather than abolishing it at once.</p>"},
      {id:"rp52",title:"Rechenplan 52",html:"<p><a href='rechenplan-52.html'>The first practical specification</a> supports static integers, fixed and floating point, fixed arrays, labels, counted repetition, conditional transfer, nonrecursive subroutines, and numerical card or tape input. It has no heap, strings, records, modules, or general recursion.</p><p>Translation is initially mixed human and machine: a Planprüfer checks forms and dimensions, a translator emits an intermediate operation list, and a coding office produces target instructions. Full automatic translation spreads during 1953–55.</p>"},
      {id:"rp56",title:"Rechenplan 56",html:"<p><a href='rechenplan-56.html'>The 1956 standard</a> becomes the first production national language. From 1957, accepted major general-purpose German computers must provide a certified compiler or documented route into <a href='planform.html'>Planform</a>.</p><p>It adds integers, fixed and floating point, truth values, characters, arrays, fixed records, stronger checking, procedures, and structured control. Static lifetime and nonrecursive procedures keep memory use predictable.</p>"},
      {id:"profiles",title:"Scientific, administrative, and control profiles",html:"<p><strong>RP-W</strong> provides matrices, complex arithmetic, numerical integration, orbital and ballistic libraries. <strong>RP-V</strong> provides exact decimals, records, keyed files, reports, dates, districts, currencies, and commodities. <strong>RP-S</strong> provides bounded loops, interrupts, devices, state tables, timing estimates, and fail-safe branches.</p><p>The profiles share syntax and compiler machinery while forbidding unsafe features where timing and memory must be certified.</p>"},
      {id:"rp63",title:"Rechenplan 63",html:"<p>ERO 63 establishes a common architectural order and Planform object system. <a href='rechenplan-63.html'>Rechenplan 63</a> responds with block scope, modules, separately compiled procedures, public and private interfaces, enumerations, stronger exceptions, and limited recursion.</p><p>Imported interfaces carry structural signatures. The linker rejects mismatched names, parameter modes, types, dimensions, exception sets, or profiles rather than silently adapting them.</p>"},
      {id:"rp68",title:"Rechenplan 68",html:"<p><a href='rechenplan-68.html'>The 1968 revision</a> adds versioned modules, channels, events, processes, controlled dynamic storage, standardized errors, cross-reference listings, and compiler/runtime identity. RP-S processes are statically created and communicate through bounded typed channels with declared overflow policy.</p><p>Its <em>Laufnachweis</em> reports timing, memory, interrupts, resource conflicts, uncertified loops, and assembly calls as engineering evidence for certification.</p>"},
      {id:"rp72",title:"Rechenplan 72",html:"<p><a href='rechenplan-72.html'>The 1972 revision</a> consolidates modules, controlled references, variant records, parameterized libraries, source/object version identifiers, messages, standard devices, and precise exception behavior. RP-V gains the read-only <a href='auswahlplan.html'><em>Auswahlplan</em></a> query sublanguage for records, grouping, ordering, and authorized joins.</p><p>Controlled references cannot perform pointer arithmetic, and their targets must outlive them. Scientific heap regions belong to modules and are reclaimed as units.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Strategic role and legacy",html:"<p>Rechenplan remains mandatory for weapons, spacecraft, nuclear plants, central MOSAIK, military communications, and automated rail. The 1982 civilian-language reform opens ordinary software to other paradigms without weakening this certified core.</p><p>Its lasting contribution is institutional: type declarations, object metadata, tests, machine generators, audit trails, and release signatures travel together as the program.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"planform.html",kicker:"Intermediate representation",label:"Planform"},{href:"planwerk.html",kicker:"Operating environment",label:"Planwerk"},{href:"german-civilian-languages.html",kicker:"Civilian successors",label:"German Civilian Languages"}],facts:[["First specification","1952"],["National production standard","Rechenplan 56"],["Major revisions","52, 56, 63, 68, 72"],["Profiles","RP-W, RP-V, RP-S"],["Intermediate form","Planform"],["Operating family","Planwerk"],["1985 strategic status","Mandatory in certified domains"],["Origin","Plankalkül and postwar German research"]]
  }),

  "planform": softwareArticle({
    slug:"planform",title:"Planform",eyebrow:"German intermediate-language standard · 1952–1985",infoboxKicker:"Portable German program representation",
    lead:"Planform is the family of typed intermediate representations connecting Rechenplan source to German machine generators. It makes source-level portability, interface checking, certification, and reproduction possible without requiring every accepted computer to share one instruction set.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-s1a2b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:05:00-06:00 -->The Planform route begins with Rechenplan 52's mixed translation system, becomes the national production path under Rechenplan 56, develops into stack-oriented Planform II with Rechenplan 63, and later produces Zivil-Planform 82.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The serialized three-address and stack-machine definitions, record layout, opcodes, frame ABI, validation suite, and other low-level format details are connective technical design.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"purpose",title:"Purpose",html:"<p>German procurement accepts architectural families rather than one universal binary machine. Planform records operations, types, calls, control flow, storage, input/output classes, and required libraries in a form from which manufacturers build validated back ends.</p><p>Precision, peripheral behavior, and some layouts remain target-specific, but their deviations must be declared rather than hidden in the compiler.</p>"},
      {id:"planform-i",title:"Planform I",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-s2c3d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:05:00-06:00 --><p>Planform I emerges in Rechenplan 52's translation route and becomes the standardized production intermediate under Rechenplan 56.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The technical reference models it as a typed three-address representation divided into procedure records. Values carry declared types and symbolic storage identities; branches target numbered basic-plan labels; calls name procedures or libraries and ordered argument descriptors.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>The modeled form has no general stack operation because the source languages do not require unrestricted recursion and allocate procedure storage statically.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"chain",title:"Compilation chain",html:"<p>The standard route is Rechenplan source to typed Planform, optimized machine plan, and native instructions. A new computer requires a generator, runtime and peripheral modules, plus a validation suite demonstrating the claimed profile.</p><p>This divides authority between the national language standard and manufacturer-specific code generation while preserving a common audit point.</p>"},
      {id:"planform-ii",title:"Planform II",html:"<p>Rechenplan 63 brings a stack-oriented Planform II influenced by Bauer–Samelson compiler work. It records evaluation stacks, procedure frames, records, structured control, exception paths, device classes, and timing or storage annotations.</p><p>Frames specify arguments, locals, maximum temporaries, saved return state, and exception continuation. Linkers compare exported and imported structural signatures.</p>"},
      {id:"certification",title:"Certification and evidence",html:"<p>Planform preserves enough information for type checking, memory mapping, timing review, interface audit, and source-level diagnostics. RP-S annotations support a Laufnachweis identifying resource conflicts, unbounded work, assembly escapes, and expected interrupt behavior.</p><p>The representation is not a mathematical proof of safety. It is standardized evidence that can be independently reviewed and reproduced.</p>"},
      {id:"zivil",title:"Zivil-Planform 82",html:"<p>The 1982 Zivilsprachenrichtlinie creates a less restrictive civilian intermediate form supporting integers, floats, strings, arrays, records, calls, graphics, files, errors, network requests, and foreign-library calls.</p><p>Werkcode, Lehrplan, Folge, Schluss, Klasse, Bildschrift, Nachricht, and Plan Austral can share services and data through Zivil-Planform without becoming dialects of one language.</p>"},
      {id:"portability",title:"Limits of portability",html:"<p>Planform normally provides source-profile and intermediate-object compatibility, not a universal executable. Machine generators still determine word width, arithmetic precision, instruction selection, storage layout, and device binding.</p><p>Even Planform programs require declared character mappings, runtime revisions, libraries, and security profiles. A surviving intermediate file without its environment is not a complete program.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Influence",html:"<p>Planform makes German manufacturers compete beneath a stable language and certification layer. It also shapes Argentine localization, Weltnetz services, and the civilian-language market after 1982.</p><p>Foreign observers alternately describe it as an unusually successful portability system and as the technical constitution of a supervised software order.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"rechenplan.html",kicker:"Source language",label:"Rechenplan"},{href:"planwerk.html",kicker:"Execution environment",label:"Planwerk"},{href:"software-portability.html",kicker:"Comparative framework",label:"Software Portability"}],facts:[["First route","Rechenplan 52"],["Production standard","With Rechenplan 56"],["First modeled form","Typed three-address Planform I"],["Second form","Stack-oriented Planform II"],["Civilian derivative","Zivil-Planform 82"],["Principal purpose","Portable generation and certification"],["Binary portability","Not generally promised"],["Low-level formats","Connective technical design"]]
  }),

  "planwerk": softwareArticle({
    slug:"planwerk",title:"Planwerk",eyebrow:"German operating-system family · 1964–1985",infoboxKicker:"Certified operating environment",
    lead:"Planwerk is Germany's standardized operating-system family for batch processing, protected execution, files, terminals, network queues, audit, and configuration control. It specifies services and object conventions across accepted machines rather than one portable binary kernel.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-s3e4f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:05:00-06:00 -->Planwerk as a specified operating-system family rather than one portable binary, its 1964, 1968, and 1972 service generations, their principal service additions, and the lunar Programmbuch certification practice are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> PLANPRÜFER, PLANBINDER, ABLAUFSPUR, SPEICHERBILD, and MASCHINENPLAN are connective names for tools whose functions are required; precise service ABIs, calls, exceptions, kernels, and drivers remain technical design or implementation-specific.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"From Planlauf to Planwerk",html:"<p>The Planlauf monitor of the late 1950s reads job identity, security class, compiler, libraries, media, output destination, and time limit; it loads the job, accounts for time, and prints diagnostics. Larger disks, protected supervisors, and multiprogramming turn this monitor into an operating family.</p>"},
      {id:"pw64",title:"Planwerk 64",html:"<p>Planwerk 64 defines protected supervisor and user states, multiprogrammed batch, queues, spooling, library loading, accounting, security classification, checkpoints, audits, remote job submission, and common object and tape formats.</p><p>It is a service contract implemented separately on ERO-family machines, not one machine-code image.</p>"},
      {id:"pw68",title:"Planwerk 68",html:"<p>The 1968 generation adds disk files, user protection, selected interactive terminal sessions, and versioned modules. Batch remains central, but programmers can inspect files, submit compiles, and debug approved noncritical work without waiting for an entire card cycle.</p>"},
      {id:"pw72",title:"Planwerk 72",html:"<p>Planwerk 72 adds network queues, replication, configuration control, detailed access logs, and device-health monitoring. These services make distributed MOSAIK work possible while keeping authority, routing, and revision provenance explicit.</p>"},
      {id:"tools",title:"Development toolchain",html:"<p>Standard tools include PLANPRÜFER for syntax and type checking, PLANBINDER for linking, ABLAUFSPUR for instrumented traces, SPEICHERBILD for maps and dumps, and target-specific MASCHINENPLAN assemblers.</p><p>The common names conceal different implementations but give programmers and auditors a predictable workflow across major systems.</p>"},
      {id:"programmbuch",title:"The Programmbuch",html:"<p>The lunar program establishes the Programmbuch: source, compiler, target, library revisions, tests, anomalies, workarounds, memory map, hardware revision, and release signatures. Flight executables are independently reviewed, simulated, frozen, reproduced bit-for-bit, and checked against signed masters.</p><p>The practice later spreads to weapons, reactors, automated rail, and central communications.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"Security and administration",html:"<p>Classification, accounting, access logs, and library control make Planwerk both a technical and administrative system. It can identify who submitted a program, which generator built it, what data it read, and where its output was delivered.</p><p>Critics regard this traceability as inseparable from political supervision; defenders call it essential to shared strategic computers.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>Planwerk remains the environment of strategic Rechenplan computing even after civilian work moves toward Zivil-Planform, workstations, and Weltnetz services. Gateways expose selected files and queues without allowing consumer systems to inherit strategic privileges.</p><p>Its combination of portability and audit becomes a model studied—though rarely copied in full—outside the German sphere.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"rechenplan.html",kicker:"Principal language",label:"Rechenplan"},{href:"planform.html",kicker:"Object system",label:"Planform"},{href:"computing-networks.html",kicker:"Network setting",label:"Computing and Global Networks"}],facts:[["Predecessor","Planlauf batch monitor"],["Major releases","1964, 1968, 1972"],["Execution model","Protected multiprogrammed services"],["Object system","Planform"],["Certification record","Programmbuch"],["Network services","Queues and replication after 1972"],["1985 role","Strategic operating family"]]
  }),

  "kurzplan": softwareArticle({
    slug:"kurzplan",title:"Kurzplan",eyebrow:"German home-computer language · introduced 1977",infoboxKicker:"Interpreted educational language",
    lead:"Kurzplan 77 is the interpreted German home and school programming language that carries familiar plan-language vocabulary onto machines too small for Rechenplan. Its compact token form, graphics, sound, controllers, and forgiving immediate environment make it the common entry point into German personal computing.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-s4g5h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:05:00-06:00 -->Kurzplan 77's introduction, new interpreted home-and-school identity, broad facilities—numbers, strings, arrays, procedures, control, graphics, sound, storage, and controllers—and relationship to German home computing are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 16-bit integer and optional 32-bit floating model, string and array rules, token machine, recursion, error recovery, normalized graphics, capability tests, ROM/RAM minima, disk cache, vendor dialects, and precise token values are connective implementation design.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"Origin",html:"<p>Late-1970s home computers cannot host the Rechenplan compiler, Planform toolchain, and certified runtime. Education ministries and manufacturers instead develop a compact interpreted language using recognizable words such as WENN, SONST, FÜR, WIEDERHOLE, EINGABE, AUSGABE, ZEICHNE, TON, and ENDE.</p><p>Kurzplan is a new language, not an RP profile.</p>"},
      {id:"model",title:"Language model",html:"<p>The portable machine uses sixteen-bit signed integers, optional thirty-two-bit floating point, length-counted strings, zero-origin arrays, conditions, loops, procedures, and a compact token stream. Variables appear on first assignment, with a suffix distinguishing strings.</p><p>Procedures possess locals and may recurse only where the host supplies sufficient stack.</p>"},
      {id:"interaction",title:"Immediate programming",html:"<p>Users type commands and program lines at an immediate prompt, run or inspect the stored program, and recover from most mistakes without reloading the machine. A runtime error stops the current command and returns to the prompt while preserving source.</p><p>This behavior gives classrooms and households a style of experimentation absent from batch Planwerk.</p>"},
      {id:"media",title:"Cassette, disk, and bytecode",html:"<p>Cassette systems store tokenized source. Disk machines may store source or Zivil-Planform bytecode, permitting faster loading and limited exchange among compatible civilian platforms.</p><p>A minimum interpreter fits in sixteen kibibytes of ROM with eight kibibytes of RAM by omitting floating point, disk, and high-resolution graphics. Complete systems usually provide thirty-two to sixty-four kibibytes.</p>"},
      {id:"graphics",title:"Graphics, sound, and control",html:"<p>Graphics use normalized integer screen coordinates mapped by each host. Sound, controllers, printers, disks, and other devices are tested as capabilities at runtime rather than assumed to exist.</p><p>These features make Kurzplan important to education, technical hobbies, animation, household records, and the early game market.</p>"},
      {id:"education",title:"Schools and households",html:"<p>Kurzplan teaches sequencing, variables, procedures, coordinate geometry, and device control without requiring English keywords. School editions emphasize guided exercises; home editions emphasize immediate commands, graphics, sound, and cassette exchange.</p><p>Listings circulate through magazines, clubs, youth programs, and unofficial copying networks.</p>"},
      {id:"ecology",title:"Relation to the civilian-language ecology",html:"<p>After 1982, Kurzplan remains the beginner and small-machine language while Lehrplan serves structured teaching, Werkcode systems work, Bildschrift graphics, and other languages professionalize civilian software.</p><p>Plan Austral borrows its interactive and graphics conveniences for Argentine machines while translating compiled work into the German civilian stack.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Cultural legacy",html:"<p>Kurzplan gives the German-speaking public a shared idea of what typing a program feels like. It also helps software escape its original institutional chains: cassettes and printed listings are harder to supervise than certified strategic executables.</p><p>The success of <a href='mauerwerk.html'><em>Mauerwerk</em></a> makes this tension visible across the bloc.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"german-civilian-languages.html",kicker:"Later ecology",label:"German Civilian Languages"},{href:"plan-austral.html",kicker:"Argentine descendant",label:"Plan Austral"},{href:"mauerwerk.html",kicker:"Home-computing culture",label:"Mauerwerk"}],facts:[["Introduced","1977"],["Implementation","Interpreted"],["Broad facilities","Numbers, strings, arrays, procedures, graphics, sound, storage, controllers"],["Portable machine model","Connective design"],["Minimum-memory figures","Connective design"],["Primary use","Home and school programming"]]
  }),

  "german-civilian-languages": softwareArticle({
    slug:"german-civilian-languages",title:"German civilian programming languages",eyebrow:"Zivilsprachenrichtlinie ecology · 1980–2010",infoboxKicker:"Civilian software-language family",
    lead:"German civilian programming languages comprise the systems, educational, functional, logic, object, graphics, specialist, and distributed languages that emerged from 1980 and entered a common policy framework under the 1982 Zivilsprachenrichtlinie. Their common rules concern interoperable services, provenance, data exchange, and separation from certified strategic control. The named language ecology and Folge's later position are incorporated technical history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Felix Hitler's early-2000s functional production-language project is directly established; its exact route to a bounded niche by 2010 is later elaboration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l8d4j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T09:05:00-06:00 -->The 1982 Zivilsprachenrichtlinie, its maxim ‘certify uses, not ideas,’ strategic Rechenplan boundary, seven principal language families, and Zivil-Planform ecology are incorporated into the consolidated Setting Bible and software reference.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The twenty-three named specialist languages, detailed dates and purposes, compiler ecology, and Folge's later position remain incorporated technical construction rather than direct authorial statements.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Felix Hitler's desire to build a German functional production language in the early 2000s, influenced by ML and Lisp and aiming toward an F#-like result, is directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its detailed phase chronology, <em>Prinzensprache</em> nickname, breakthrough application, and bounded adoption are incorporated elaboration; authorship, institutional homes, compiler and user totals, complete grammars, surviving firms, and the final public name remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"directive",title:"The 1982 directive",html:"<p>The Zivilsprachenrichtlinie adopts the formula “certify uses, not ideas.” It ends the assumption that all respectable software must be written in a Rechenplan profile while preserving mandatory certification for weapons, spacecraft, nuclear plants, central MOSAIK, military communications, and automated rail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"substrate",title:"Zivil-Planform substrate",html:"<p>Zivil-Planform 82 supplies common integers, floating point, strings, arrays, records, calls, graphics, files, errors, network requests, and foreign-library calls. Languages may disagree about syntax, typing, objects, and evaluation while exchanging declared modules and services.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"families",title:"The seven principal families",html:"<p>The principal general-purpose and paradigm-defining families are incorporated by name, date, purpose, and intellectual lineage in the consolidated software record.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Date</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Principal influences</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href='werkcode.html'>Werkcode</a></td><td>1980</td><td>Systems and microcomputers</td><td>C and BCPL</td></tr><tr><td><a href='lehrplan.html'>Lehrplan</a></td><td>1981</td><td>Structured education and business</td><td>Pascal and ALGOL</td></tr><tr><td><a href='folge.html'>Folge</a></td><td>1981</td><td>Typed functional programming</td><td>ML and Lambda</td></tr><tr><td><a href='schluss.html'>Schluss</a></td><td>1982</td><td>Rules and diagnosis</td><td>Prolog</td></tr><tr><td><a href='klasse.html'>Klasse</a></td><td>1982</td><td>Objects, simulation, and graphics</td><td>Simula and Smalltalk</td></tr><tr><td><a href='bildschrift.html'>Bildschrift</a></td><td>1983</td><td>Windows, animation, and games</td><td>Graphical scene systems</td></tr><tr><td><a href='nachricht.html'>Nachricht</a></td><td>1984</td><td>Messages and distributed programs</td><td>Process and message research</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"specialist",title:"Specialist and small-system languages",html:"<p>The seven principal families are not the complete civilian inventory. The consolidated technical reconstruction also names the following specialist, office, educational, hobby, publishing, control, research, and media languages by date and purpose; both the roster and detailed semantics are incorporated design rather than direct authorial registry.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Named languages</th><th>Principal uses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1980</td><td><a href='schnur.html'>Schnur</a> · <a href='kleinplan.html'>Kleinplan</a> · <a href='heimplan-s.html'>Heimplan-S</a> · <a href='heimplan-t.html'>Heimplan-T</a> · <a href='rechentafel.html'>Rechentafel</a></td><td>Electronics, small and home computers, engineering calculators</td></tr><tr><td>1981</td><td><a href='bericht.html'>Bericht</a> · <a href='spielsatz.html'>Spielsatz</a> · <a href='satzwerk.html'>Satzwerk</a> · <a href='formblatt.html'>Formblatt</a> · <a href='zeichenweg.html'>Zeichenweg</a></td><td>Reports, games, publishing, forms, and school geometry</td></tr><tr><td>1982</td><td><a href='schaltfolge.html'>Schaltfolge</a> · <a href='wortbruecke.html'>Wortbrücke</a> · <a href='datenfrage.html'>Datenfrage</a> · <a href='klangfolge.html'>Klangfolge</a> · <a href='geraetewort.html'>Gerätewort</a></td><td>Civilian control, localization, databases, music, and laboratories</td></tr><tr><td>1983</td><td><a href='netzblatt.html'>Netzblatt</a> · <a href='baukasten.html'>Baukasten</a> · <a href='kartenwerk.html'>Kartenwerk</a> · <a href='reihe.html'>Reihe</a> · <a href='beweis.html'>Beweis</a></td><td>Information services, visual teaching, mapping, arrays, and theorem work</td></tr><tr><td>1984</td><td><a href='werkstatt.html'>Werkstatt</a> · <a href='handelssatz.html'>Handelssatz</a> · <a href='fernbild.html'>Fernbild</a></td><td>Build automation, retail and inventory, videotex and remote graphics</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"paradigms",title:"Imported ideas and German forms",html:"<p>Each family is fitted to German character orders, libraries, regulated-device boundaries, and Zivil-Planform calls rather than reproducing a foreign execution environment.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"market",title:"Software houses and publication",html:"<p>The directive permits specialist firms, university groups, publishers, and equipment makers to maintain distinct languages and tools. Provenance records identify compiler, runtime, interface, and exporter without requiring central approval of every civilian algorithm.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>This produces a rapid software-house ecology after 1982, though access to strategic interfaces remains licensed.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"boundary",title:"The strategic boundary",html:"<p>A civilian program may prepare data, visualize results, request a network service, or call an approved library. It may not silently acquire authority over a weapon, reactor, railway interlock, classified network, or spacecraft control path.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Gateways enforce this boundary through declared capabilities, conversion, logging, and supervised service calls.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"debate",title:"Political and technical debate",html:"<p>Reformers argue that plurality attracts ideas and programmers that the certified establishment had excluded. Conservatives warn that incompatible languages weaken training and make audit harder. Security offices focus less on syntax than on provenance, export, and device authority.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>The policy liberalizes civilian software development while retaining a bounded national software order.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"endpoint",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 the seven principal languages are recognizable but uneven institutions. Some possess several compilers and active publishers; others remain concentrated in research or one industrial network.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Kurzplan continues as the widespread household language, while Rechenplan remains mandatory in certified strategic domains.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"later",title:"Later development",html:"<p>Folge remains a smaller language for research, education, compilers, and symbolic work after 1985. It does not displace Rechenplan, Werkcode, or Klasse in their established fields.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Felix Wolfgang Hitler's early-2000s functional production-language project is directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A compiler project around 2002–04, the nickname <em>Prinzensprache</em>, and a major Raumhafen or Mars-logistics application giving it a bounded technical niche by 2010 are incorporated chronology. Its final public name remains open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"planform.html",kicker:"Shared substrate",label:"Zivil-Planform"},{href:"kurzplan.html",kicker:"Household language",label:"Kurzplan"},{href:"computing-networks.html",kicker:"Network setting",label:"Computing and Global Networks"}],facts:[["Policy basis","Zivilsprachenrichtlinie, 1982 <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Governing maxim","Certify uses, not ideas <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Shared substrate","Zivil-Planform 82 <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Principal families","Seven <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Named specialist languages","Twenty-three through 1984 <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Strategic-language boundary","Rechenplan remains mandatory <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Newest principal family by 1985","Nachricht 84 <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Later functional project","Author-established project; detailed c. 2000–2010 path incorporated <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Primary market","Civilian German software <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"]]
  }),

  "plan-austral": softwareArticle({
    slug:"plan-austral",title:"Plan Austral",eyebrow:"Argentine programming language · 1982",infoboxKicker:"Spanish-language civilian system",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-s5j6k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:05:00-06:00 --><strong>Plan Austral 82</strong> is Argentina's Spanish-language programming system over the German civilian software stack. Its Spanish surface, Kurzplan and Zivil-Planform descent, and German-derived data, modules, files, calls, errors, Rechenplan linkage, and Weltnetz messaging are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A structured Lehrplan-derived compiled form paired with an untyped Kurzplan-like immediate shell is the incorporated technical design.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-s6m7n writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:05:00-06:00 -->Plan Austral's 1982 date, Spanish surface and keywords, descent from Kurzplan and Zivil-Planform, localization role, and German-derived data and service environment are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Static typing for compiled modules, an untyped immediate shell, Lehrplan 81 as the compiled basis, exact translation guarantees, graphics and string conveniences, institutional authorship, and full grammar remain connective design or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"Background",html:"<p>Argentina's German-assisted computer industry needs local education and commercial software without abandoning imported libraries, diagnostics, or network interfaces. Direct use of German source proves politically awkward and pedagogically costly.</p><p>Plan Austral answers by localizing the source language while preserving the licensed technical stack beneath it.</p>"},
      {id:"surface",title:"Spanish source form",html:"<p>Representative words include SI, SINO, PARA, MIENTRAS, LEER, ESCRIBIR, PROCEDIMIENTO, REGISTRO, and FIN. Comments, messages, documentation, and user identifiers can remain Spanish throughout development.</p><p>The language is not a mere keyword substitution: its teaching shell and graphics conventions are designed for Argentine schools and personal systems.</p>"},
      {id:"compiled",title:"Compiled language",html:"<p>Compiled modules are statically typed and follow a Spanish surface dialect of Lehrplan 81. They provide block scope, records, procedures, structured control, files, graphics conveniences, errors, and declared foreign calls.</p><p>Compilation produces Zivil-Planform-compatible modules for German-derived runtimes and libraries.</p>"},
      {id:"shell",title:"Immediate shell",html:"<p>An untyped immediate shell allows calculations, experiments, short graphics, and educational commands without a complete compiled module. This Kurzplan-like layer lowers the barrier to classroom and home use.</p><p>Serious applications can transfer stable routines into the typed compiled form.</p>"},
      {id:"translation",title:"Lehrplan interchange",html:"<p>A canonical translator converts declarations and statements losslessly between Plan Austral and normalized Lehrplan, except for comments and user-selected identifier spelling. This lets Argentine firms localize source while retaining German libraries and diagnostics.</p><p>The translation contract is source-profile portability, not a promise that every German executable runs unchanged.</p>"},
      {id:"uses",title:"Education and industry",html:"<p>Schools use the shell and structured subset; firms use compiled modules for records, production planning, engineering utilities, graphical displays, and communications. German technical manuals are translated alongside interface declarations.</p><p>The language becomes a visible symbol of national competence within a dependent technical relationship.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Politics of dependence",html:"<p>Supporters present Plan Austral as proof that alignment does not require linguistic submission. Critics note that object formats, runtime services, advanced libraries, and Weltnetz links remain German-derived.</p><p>Its autonomy is therefore real at the source and application level but bounded at the platform and strategic-system level.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Plan Austral is established in education and selected commercial work, though imported German systems continue to dominate high-end engineering and defense. Its success encourages other associated states to seek localized source without demanding independent hardware ecosystems.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"argentina.html",kicker:"National setting",label:"Argentina"},{href:"kurzplan.html",kicker:"Interactive ancestor",label:"Kurzplan"},{href:"german-civilian-languages.html",kicker:"Technical substrate",label:"German Civilian Languages"}],facts:[["Introduced","1982"],["Country","Argentina"],["Source language","Spanish"],["Established descent","Kurzplan and Zivil-Planform"],["Object environment","German-derived"],["Network environment","Weltnetz-derived messaging"],["Compiled and shell design","Connective technical model"]]
  }),

  "software-portability": softwareArticle({
    slug:"software-portability",title:"Software portability",eyebrow:"Cross-bloc standards and translation · 1947–1985",infoboxKicker:"Comparative software practice",
    lead:"Software portability is the preservation of a program's useful behavior when it moves between computers, operating environments, institutions, languages, or political blocs. A portable algorithm may still require rewritten source; portable source may still depend upon unavailable libraries; a shared intermediate object may still require a new code generator; and a program using identical text may still fail because its numbers, files, characters, devices, or timing have changed. By 1985 portability is an established engineering and commercial field, but there is no neutral universal application platform.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>",
    canon:"Incompatible hardware and character systems, Germany's source-and-intermediate portability, the Commonwealth's leading translation culture, American vendor and Bridge markets, Japanese procedural and text standards, Latin multilingual exchange, UNICODE 80's abstract-identity role, extensive cross-bloc language survival, and the absence of a universal 1985 platform are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The five-level taxonomy, detailed Bridge application interface, precise processor-profile guarantees, standardized porting dossier, and several workflow descriptions are incorporated connective technical design.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>",
    sections:[
      {id:"levels",title:"Compatibility levels",html:"<p>The following five-level vocabulary is used in technical comparison. It is a connective classification rather than the title of one canonically established international standard.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>What survives</th><th>Work still required</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Algorithmic</td><td>The method, formula, or procedure can be understood and reexpressed.</td><td>The program may be rewritten completely for a new language and machine.</td></tr><tr><td>Source-family</td><td>Grammar and broad semantics are substantially shared.</td><td>Libraries, data, operating calls, dialects, and machine assumptions may require changes.</td></tr><tr><td>Source-profile</td><td>Conforming source and its declared environment compile without edits.</td><td>A compiler, runtime, libraries, and target profile must exist on the destination.</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate or object</td><td>A typed intermediate form, object format, or declared application interface is shared.</td><td>The destination still requires a binder, code generator, runtime, adapters, or compatible services.</td></tr><tr><td>Binary</td><td>The same executable and required runtime operate unchanged.</td><td>Hardware and environment must meet the promised binary profile.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>“Compatible” without a stated level, profile, and environment is an incomplete claim. Binary portability is the strongest and least common form.</p>"},
      {id:"components",title:"Components of a port",html:"<p>A port begins with more than source text. Engineers identify source edition, compiler, target machine, word and byte rules, arithmetic precision, array and record layout, file structure, libraries, runtime, operating services, peripheral calls, display behavior, timing assumptions, and data encoding. Documentation, tests, build procedures, and operator instructions may be as important as the program statements.</p><p>Libraries and data frequently cost more to move than an algorithm. A payroll program may compile correctly while reading packed decimals incorrectly. A scientific program may produce plausible but different results after a change in rounding or floating-point range. A control program may miss a deadline even when every calculation is numerically correct.</p>"},
      {id:"hardware",title:"Hardware and operating environment",html:"<p>The postwar computer industries retain incompatible instruction sets, word sizes, byte orders, arithmetic representations, address ranges, channels, peripheral protocols, and protection mechanisms. Separate compilation creates reusable modules inside one system; it does not create binary compatibility across unrelated machines.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><p>Operating environments add another boundary. File naming, record access, terminal behavior, job control, error handling, memory protection, networking, clocks, printers, graphics, and device drivers are exposed differently. A language standard can isolate these services behind libraries or profiles, but an implementation still has to supply them.</p>"},
      {id:"characters",title:"Text and character systems",html:"<p>American ASCII and EBCDIC, German six- and twelve-bit codes, Japanese compact and multibyte registries, Latin eight-bit text, and diverse British machines disagree about character identity, source entry, collation, and file representation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Keyword translation alone cannot preserve identifiers, accented names, vertical Japanese records, sorting rules, printer glyphs, or external data.</p><p>The 1980 <a href='unicode-80.html'>UNICODE 80</a> standard assigns sixteen-bit abstract character identities and transfer mappings. It does not prescribe one processor word, mandatory file encoding, font, keyboard, collation sequence, language syntax, or executable format.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Japan and the Latin bloc enforce it early; British adoption is opportunistic, German adoption follows later, and decentralized American practice converges mainly during the 1990s.</p>"},
      {id:"chronology",title:"Development",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Development</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1947–1955</td><td>Libraries, symbolic coding, autocodes, and early translators move procedures among small incompatible machines. German Rechenplan develops an unusually explicit source-portability model.</td></tr><tr><td>1955–1965</td><td>FORTRAN, COBOL, ALGOL, Rechenplan, Common Autocode, KEISAN, TEJUN, and Latin systems separate algorithms and source from individual instruction sets. Compatible machine families and batch operating systems enlarge the practical target.</td></tr><tr><td>1965–1975</td><td>Modules, linkers, typed intermediate forms, protected operating environments, minicomputers, and packet research make libraries and services central portability questions.</td></tr><tr><td>1975–1980</td><td>Home computers produce a large market of nominally shared BASIC and Pascal families whose machine dialects, graphics, storage, and peripherals remain incompatible.</td></tr><tr><td>1980–1985</td><td>UNICODE 80, Bridge/16, Zivil-Planform, language-exchange institutions, and cross-bloc networks make portability a routine contractual and industrial concern.</td></tr><tr><td>Late 1980s–2000</td><td>Weltnetz becomes the common international network layer and Bridge the leading commercial compatibility environment, while local processors, operating systems, applications, and strategic networks persist.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"german",title:"German source and intermediate portability",html:"<p><a href='rechenplan.html'>Rechenplan</a> begins by moving explicitly declared calculations among Zuse, Göttingen, and Telefunken machines. A program travels when its numeric assumptions are declared, required libraries exist, machine-specific input and output are isolated, and the destination provides sufficient precision. Translation remains expensive in the 1950s because some stages are performed by coding offices and human reviewers.</p><p><a href='planform.html'>Planform II</a> later supplies typed operations, procedure frames, records, control flow, exceptions, device classes, and resource annotations. A manufacturer seeking access to the German software library provides a Planform code generator, standard runtime, peripheral modules, and validation tests. <a href='planform.html'>Zivil-Planform 82</a> extends a related substrate to civilian languages. The system normally promises source-profile and intermediate portability, not one executable for every German machine.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"commonwealth",title:"Commonwealth translation practice",html:"<p>Britain becomes the principal compiler-compiler, cross-bloc source translator, and gateway culture.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Early programme libraries serve incompatible Manchester, Cambridge, commercial, and Commonwealth machines. British institutions learn to separate a method from its source dialect, machine edition, data layout, and operating procedure.</p><p>A representative porting dossier identifies the source dialect, numeric and character profile, compiler and library editions, target services, substituted routines, conversion tools, conformance tests, performance differences, and known deviations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> British translators carry ALGOL, COBOL, C, Prolog, ML, Pascal, and other lineages between environments while preserving local documentation and audit requirements.</p>"},
      {id:"american",title:"American vendor compatibility",html:"<p>American portability develops through commercial language standards, compatible product families, operating systems, and a competitive software market. FORTRAN offers a widely exchanged scientific source family. COBOL formalizes machine-independent business records. C and UNIX tools make systems source relatively movable among institutions willing to reproduce the environment. Vendor extensions and incompatible libraries remain common.</p><p><a href='bridge-operating-environment.html'>Bridge</a> is a licensed portable operating environment organized around stable interfaces, translators, and software survival across replaceable hardware.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The connective Bridge/16 model promises source-profile portability across declared processor profiles and binary portability within one profile. Drivers, byte order, extended arithmetic, device capability, and version requirements remain visible parts of the contract.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"japanese",title:"Japanese procedural compatibility",html:"<p>Japanese computing combines vertically integrated manufacturers with common imperial character and procedural requirements. KEISAN source, TEJUN control practice, native text registries, standard forms, and tightly managed hardware-service combinations support movement inside a defined family. Cross-vendor and foreign movement normally requires translators or gateways.</p><p>UNICODE 80 removes one major barrier to international data identity. Japanese adoption does not eliminate vertical writing, local sorting, specialized device control, or manufacturer-specific operating services. The Japanese model favors a complete tested procedure transferred with its required equipment, records, operators, and maintenance instructions.</p>"},
      {id:"latin",title:"Latin multilingual exchange",html:"<p>The Latin bloc uses ALGOL as a publication and scientific language, Pascal for education, Prolog for symbolic and rule-based work, Ada for military and aerospace systems, and a mixture of LIA, C, Modula-like systems, and Occitan control languages in production. Shared language ancestry does not create one Latin executable or operating system.</p><p>Italian, Spanish, Portuguese, Occitan, and Latin documentation requires explicit character and terminology mapping. UNICODE 80 and common interfaces help source, data, and modules travel among Olivetti, Occitan, Spanish, Portuguese, and foreign systems. Operational control profiles remain machine- and project-specific.</p>"},
      {id:"languages",title:"Cross-bloc language survival",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Language or idea</th><th>Principal portable inheritance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assembly and macro assemblers</td><td>Universal low-level method; source and object remain target-specific.</td></tr><tr><td>FORTRAN</td><td>American-led scientific source family with extensive foreign implementations.</td></tr><tr><td>COBOL</td><td>Institutional records, decimal arithmetic, reports, and commercial source exchange.</td></tr><tr><td>ALGOL</td><td>Machine-independent algorithm publication and the structured-language lineage.</td></tr><tr><td>BASIC</td><td>International household and teaching vocabulary fragmented into machine dialects.</td></tr><tr><td>Pascal and Modula</td><td>Portable teaching and structured systems practice, especially in Latin and Commonwealth markets.</td></tr><tr><td>Simula</td><td>Norwegian simulation and object concepts adopted internationally.</td></tr><tr><td>C and UNIX tools</td><td>American systems source transported through Britain, Bridge, Germany, Japan, and Latin environments.</td></tr><tr><td>Prolog</td><td>Occitan logic programming with British cooperation and worldwide research use.</td></tr><tr><td>ML</td><td>British/Commonwealth typed functional and theorem-proving lineage.</td></tr><tr><td>Ada</td><td>American-led multinational military and aerospace source standard.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A shared language name establishes ancestry, not a common edition, runtime, library, data format, or executable.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"},
      {id:"validation",title:"Testing and certification",html:"<p>Moving a validated scientific, financial, aerospace, rail, reactor, or weapons program creates a new technical artifact. Compiler changes can alter arithmetic, memory use, error behavior, and timing. A port is tested against reference cases, boundary conditions, stored results, data conversions, resource limits, failure states, and operational procedures.</p><p>German practice records compiler, generator, runtime, target, library, test, anomaly, and release signatures in the <em>Programmbuch</em>. Commonwealth and American institutions use different paperwork and legal forms, but regulated customers likewise require versioned source, test records, acceptance criteria, and named responsibility. Interactive or consumer programs accept wider variation than certified control software.</p>"},
      {id:"rights",title:"Licensing and implementation rights",html:"<p>Technical possibility does not establish legal permission. A customer may possess an executable yet lack source code, translator rights, library documentation, hardware-interface rights, or authority to make a foreign machine edition. Porting contracts distinguish use, adaptation, translation, distribution, maintenance, and access to proprietary tests and interfaces.</p><p><a href='software-licensing-contracts.html'>Software licensing contracts</a>, source escrow, chain-of-title records, and <a href='software-hardware-implementation-rights.html'>hardware-implementation rights</a> become important as firms sell continuing capability rather than a single tape. Export and security rules can forbid a technically straightforward port to a rival bloc.</p>"},
      {id:"networks",title:"Networks and gateways",html:"<p>Packet networks permit heterogeneous machines to exchange messages without running the same applications. Gateways translate character sets, records, protocols, authentication, names, and service calls. Network compatibility therefore becomes separable from processor and application compatibility.</p><p>By the late 1980s and 1990s <a href='weltnetz.html'>Weltnetz</a> becomes the common international network layer through German–Japanese convergence, UNICODE 80, satellite routing, local caching, and Bridge conversion.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> It does not replace bloc processors, operating systems, files, applications, search systems, or protected strategic networks. By 2000 Bridge is the leading commercial compatibility environment while Concord remains a prestigious closed ecosystem.</p>"},
      {id:"comparison",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Principal portability mechanism</th><th>Persistent limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Germany</td><td>Validated Rechenplan profiles, Planform, and later Zivil-Planform.</td><td>Certification, device authority, and target generators remain institutionally controlled.</td></tr><tr><td>British Commonwealth</td><td>Compiler-compilers, source translation, documented libraries, and gateway services.</td><td>Fragmented hardware and limited production scale require continuing adaptation.</td></tr><tr><td>United States</td><td>Commercial standards, compatible families, C/UNIX source, and Bridge profiles.</td><td>Vendor APIs, extensions, and market competition fragment the environment.</td></tr><tr><td>Japan</td><td>Integrated manufacturer systems, procedural standards, and strict character registries.</td><td>Vertical stacks and native devices complicate foreign and cross-vendor ports.</td></tr><tr><td>Latin bloc</td><td>International languages, multilingual text, and negotiated common interfaces.</td><td>Four national industries and specialized control systems retain distinct implementations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Portability reduces dependence on one machine while sometimes increasing dependence on the institution controlling the language profile, intermediate form, runtime, validation suite, or gateway. Software exchange is extensive by 1985; no body controls a universal platform.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"planform.html",kicker:"German model",label:"Planform"},{href:"bridge-operating-environment.html",kicker:"American model",label:"Bridge"},{href:"unicode-80.html",kicker:"Character identity",label:"UNICODE 80"},{href:"commonwealth-programming-languages.html",kicker:"Translation culture",label:"Commonwealth Programming Languages"},{href:"software-licensing-contracts.html",kicker:"Commercial instrument",label:"Software Licensing Contracts"},{href:"software-hardware-implementation-rights.html",kicker:"Legal boundary",label:"Hardware-Implementation Rights"},{href:"weltnetz.html",kicker:"Later network layer",label:"Weltnetz"}],facts:[["Compatibility taxonomy","Five levels · connective classification <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Strongest level","Binary portability"],["Weakest level","Algorithmic portability"],["German model","Rechenplan source profiles and Planform generators <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["American market model","Bridge profiles; detailed guarantees connective <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Translation leader","British Commonwealth <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Character aid","UNICODE 80 <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Common international network layer","Weltnetz by the late 1980s–1990s <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Leading commercial environment by 2000","Bridge <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Universal 1985 application platform","None <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"]]
  })
});

